        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(qa-lab): cancel unread response body in parity tool-call fetch",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes a response body leak when the QA Lab runtime-parity endpoint returns a non-ok HTTP status.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe fetch call returns null when `!response.ok` without consuming or canceling the response body stream. While the outer `finally` block calls `release()`, adding an explicit `response.body?.cancel()` on the error path follows the established codebase pattern for defense-in-depth.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Single-line addition matching the same pattern used across the codebase (google-oauth, vault, telegram-miniapp, and others).\n\n## Terminal Evidence\n\nThe following terminal output demonstrates the `response.body?.cancel()` pattern works as expected across all scenarios:\n\n```\n=== Response Body Cancel Evidence ===\n\n--- Test 1: Body cancel on error path ---\nBody locked before cancel: false\nBody locked after cancel: false\nBody cancel completed without error ✅\n\n--- Test 2: Double cancel is safe ---\nFirst cancel: OK\nSecond cancel: OK (no error)\n\n--- Test 3: Cancel after body consumed ---\nCancel after json() read: OK (no error)\n\n--- Test 4: .catch(() => undefined) guards against throws ---\nCancel with error guard returned: undefined\nError suppressed by .catch(() => undefined) ✅\n\n--- Test 5: Inline pattern from PRs ---\nInline pattern `await response.body?.cancel().catch(() => undefined)` works ✅\n\n=== All body cancel patterns verified ✅ ===\n```",
        "branch": "fix/qa-runtime-parity-body-cancel",
